Carry a written watermark in the fork length.

A block object that the store does not have reads as zeros, and zeros
are the right answer for a block postgres extended and never wrote:
that is a hole in a file and a new and empty page is what md gives
back for one. They are also what a page that went missing reads as, so
a table that lost a block comes up short by exactly the rows it held
and nothing anywhere says a word.

Telling the two apart needs to know which blocks have definitely had a
page reach the store. That is what this adds: dense, a block count
every block below which has been written, and filled, a bitmap of the
64 blocks above it. The pair rides in the fork's SIZE object, which
grows from four bytes to sixteen, and a four byte object still reads
as a fork claiming nothing, which is what a store written before this
has and what keeps it readable.

The shared relsize table is where the watermark has to live while the
cluster runs, because an ordinary page writeback is the one event that
proves a block was written and it settles no length. It has nowhere
else to leave the fact, so the table takes it and the checkpoint hands
both halves to the store together.

The window is there because postgres extends a relation in runs and
the backends that take the blocks write them back in whatever order
the buffer pool evicts them. Waiting for block dense itself would
stall the watermark on the one block of a run that happened to be used
last.

Nothing here is exact and nothing here has to be. A watermark behind
the truth costs detection of a lost page and never accuses a healthy
fork, so a fresh entry seeds from what the store last said, a write
too far ahead of dense to fit the window is dropped, and the page
service path claims nothing at all, because its checkpointer puts are
elided and a claim over an elided put would have the fork vouch for
objects nobody wrote.

See zou #546.

